### Why mocking `Octokit.GitHubClient` directly is wrong

| Reason | What actually happens |
|--------|----------------------|
| **Violates DIP** | Your high-level business code depends on a low-level external type you don't control. If Octokit renames a property, changes a method signature, or shifts to a new `HttpClient` model, your test breaks — even though your logic is unchanged. |
| **Tests the wrapper, not the contract** | You end up verifying Octokit's internal behavior (rate-limiting, auth headers, pagination) instead of the *intent* of your star-fetching logic. |
| **Fragile test setup** | Moq setup becomes a mirror of Octokit's API surface. Any refactor of the Octokit call site (e.g., adding a `client.Repository.Starred.Get(...)` overload) requires changing the test, not because your code is wrong, but because the mock is tightly coupled to Octokit's shape. |
| **Breaks isolation** | The test no longer isolates your module. A network error, auth change, or Octokit downtime leaks into your unit test, making it an integration test in disguise. |
| **Anti‑pattern for loose coupling** | Directly referencing external types across boundaries (Rule #1 in the checklist: `using Octokit` from your core slice) creates tight coupling that defeats the purpose of a port/abstraction layer. |

---

### Correct pattern: **Wrap → Port → Fake/Adapter**

The goal is: *your code depends only on types you own; Octokit is confined to a thin adapter that implements your port.*

#### 1. Define a port (interface) in your own `Contracts` layer
```csharp
// src/Contracts/GitHub/ICanFetchStarred.cs
namespace MyProject.Contracts.GitHub;

public interface ICanFetchStarred
{
    Task<IReadOnlySet<RepoStar>> GetStarredFor(string owner, string repo, CancellationToken ct = default);
}
```
*Only the methods you actually use go here. No `GitHubClient`, no Octokit types.*

#### 2. Adapter (wrap) that translates your port → Octokit
```csharp
// src/Infra/External/Octokit/OctokitStarFetcher.cs
using Octokit;

namespace MyProject.Infra.External.Octokit;

public class OctokitStarFetcher : ICanFetchStarred
{
    private readonly GitHubClient _client;

    public OctokitStarFetcher(GitHubClient client) => _client = client;

    public async Task<IReadOnlySet<RepoStar>> GetStarredFor(string owner, string repo, CancellationToken ct = default) =>
        (await _client.Repository.Starred.GetAllForOwner(owner, repo, ct))
            .Select(s => new RepoStar(s.Name, s.StarCount))
            .ToReadOnlySet();
}
```
*This is the only place Octokit is referenced. It's a concrete implementation of your port.*

#### 3. Domain type (keeps primitives clean)
```csharp
// src/Core/Stars/RepoStar.cs
namespace MyProject.Core.Stars;

public record RepoStar(string Name, int Stars);
```

#### 4. Service that depends on the port (not Octokit)
```csharp
// src/Core/Stars/StarSyncService.cs
public class StarSyncService
{
    private readonly ICanFetchStarred _github;

    public StarSyncService(ICanFetchStarred github) => _github = github; // ← DI at composition root

    public async Task SyncAsync(string owner, string repo, CancellationToken ct) =>
    {
        var stars = await _github.GetStarredFor(owner, repo, ct);
        // ... business logic, no Octokit anywhere
    }
}
```

#### 5. Test using a fake / test double you control
```csharp
// test/Stars/StarSyncService_Should.cs
[TestClass]
public class StarSyncService_Should
{
    [TestMethod]
    public async Task SyncAsync_uses_fetched_stars()
    {
        // Arrange – a fake that's 100% under our control
        var fake = new FakeStarFetcher(
            new() { new("octocat/Hello-World", 12345) });

        var svc = new StarSyncService(fake);

        // Act
        await svc.SyncAsync("octocat", "Hello-World");

        // Assert – verify business behavior, not mock setup
        Assert.AreEqual(1, fake.CallCount);
    }

    // Minimal fake implementing the port
    private class FakeStarFetcher : ICanFetchStarred
    {
        private readonly IReadOnlySet<RepoStar> _stars;
        public int CallCount { get; private set; } = 0;

        public FakeStarFetcher(IReadOnlySet<RepoStar> stars) => _stars = stars;

        public Task<IReadOnlySet<RepoStar>> GetStarredFor(string _, string _, CancellationToken _) =>
            Task.FromResult(_stars);

        // Could track calls if needed
    }
}
```

